{"entity": "Wuhan Xingtu Xinke Electronics Co., Ltd.", "type": "organization", "description": "Wuhan Xingtu Xinke Electronics Co., Ltd. is a Chinese joint-stock company that is pursuing an initial public offering (IPO) on the Science and Technology Innovation Board of the Shanghai Stock Exchange, as evidenced by its issuance of an A-share prospectus (招股意向书). The prospectus covers issuance overview, risk factors, basic company information, business and technology, corporate governance, and financial data, along with planned fundraising projects, listing standards, share type, number of shares, and pricing. The company's registered capital increased to 55.20 million yuan in August 2018. Before the issuance, its shareholder equity structure and related-party relationships were disclosed, with no state-owned, foreign, or strategic investor shares. Chen Shengliang, Cheng Jiezhen, and Ren Qing have held various positions, and the company has a supervisory board with three members. The issuer bears issuance expenses including sponsor fees, underwriting fees, audit and capital verification fees, lawyer fees, information disclosure fees, and issuance handling fees, and has signed labor contracts and confidentiality agreements with directors, supervisors, senior management, and core technical personnel.\n\nThe company independently develops audio-video integrated service platforms and video command and control systems, based on network communication, with a focus on technological and product innovation in the audio-video field. Its core technologies include audio-video middleware, network adaptation in complex environments, and low-latency codec. Main products include video command and control systems (such as the Networked Video Command System and Display Control System) and video early-warning control systems, as well as codecs, servers, and terminal equipment. It designs and produces embedded hardware products such as codecs, servers, and terminal equipment, with self-developed software and logic circuits. Its products are primarily used in national defense command information systems and military applications, with a small amount extending to civil communication fields such as prisons, oil fields, municipal construction, and financial business sectors. The company is involved in the national defense and military equipment industry and operates in the national defense informationization sector.\n\nThe company's revenues and profits are highly concentrated in military clients, and it is subject to related risks. It sells products to military customers and integrators, and participates in military networked video command systems. Its accounts receivable, asset impairment losses, and seasonal revenue fluctuations are disclosed in the report, with long collection cycles and low accounts receivable turnover. The company follows a make-to-order production model, pays advance payments per contract terms, arranges production tasks accordingly, and self-produces core equipment. Its prospectus also discusses the development history of computer communication technology."}
